---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const zonesHref = toolPathBySlug('heart-rate-zone-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you estimate VO2 max here? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Enter a twelve-minute distance, or an age and a resting pulse. The hero is one of those methods.
      Reading a Cooper estimate from a 2.4 km twelve-minute run gives 42.4 ml/kg/min.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leave age and pulse blank and the Cooper number stands alone.
      Fill them in and the other method appears as its own card, not as a replacement.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The VO2 Max Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-vo2-is">
    <h2>What is VO2 max? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      VO2 max is oxygen use at hard work, in millilitres per kilogram per minute.
      A lab cart measures it with a mask. This page does not. It runs two published field estimates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cooper, JAMA 1968, uses the metres you cover in twelve minutes.
      Subtract 504.9, then divide by 44.73. That is the whole Cooper line.
      Uth, 2004, uses 15.3 times a max heart rate divided by the resting pulse.
    </p>
    <p>
      A cooper test calculator turns a twelve-minute distance into a number because the run is the measurement.
      For example, estimate aerobic capacity from 2.4 km in twelve minutes.
      Input: 2.4 kilometres covered in those twelve minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Output: 42.4 ml/kg/min on an aerobic capacity calculator.
      What happens when a distance in metres is typed into the kilometre field is a rejection, not a pulse result.
      vo2max from resting heart rate stays a separate card, and a fitness age calculator is a different page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="two-methods">
    <h2>What is the difference between the Cooper test and the resting pulse? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The difference between the Cooper test and the resting-pulse estimate is what you measured.
      One number comes from a run. The other comes from age and a pulse at rest.
      Treating the Cooper number and the pulse number as one measurement hides that split.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comparing that run with a resting pulse of 60 at age 30 puts Cooper at 42.4 and Uth at 47.7.
      The gap is above 5 ml/kg/min, so the note names both. A resting pulse of 68 at the same age sits closer, and the note stays quiet.
      Leaving one method blank so the other stands alone is the way to read a single test.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="not-fitness-age">
    <h2>Does this page compute fitness age? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      No. The page does not compute fitness age. It also does not diagnose, and it does not replace a lab cart.
      Max heart rate, when the pulse method runs, is Tanaka 2001: 208 minus 0.7 times age.
    </p>
    <p>
      Training zones from age are a different job, on the <a href={zonesHref}>heart rate zone calculator</a>.
      A blank distance with age 30 and a resting pulse of 50 makes Uth the hero, about 57.2.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes">
    <h2>Which VO2 max mistakes reject the run? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Metres in the kilometre field">
      <p>
        Typing the distance in metres while the field is in kilometres, such as 2400, is rejected.
        The message names kilometres and 2.4 km. In miles it names 1.5 miles. The pulse method does not fill in after that error.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Two methods, one label">
      <p>
        Treating the Cooper number and the pulse number as one measurement drops the method name.
        The hero says Cooper or Uth. The other card, when it exists, keeps the second name.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Fitness age">
      <p>
        Expecting a fitness age from this page will not work. There is no age-equivalent output.
        The word vo2max on the result stays tied to the method that produced it.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="One method, or both named">
      <p>
        Use the distance, or the pulse, or both with both labels.
        A gap of 5 ml/kg/min is a disagreement, not a rounding error.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Field estimates, on your device">
      <p>
        Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
        A Cooper window runs from 0.8 km to 6 km. Outside that, the distance is not a twelve-minute result.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
